  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 PM EDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\n- Rain tapers off this afternoon/evening \n\nPrecipitation is in the process of winding down this afternoon over \nSouthern Lower Michigan. A zone of convergence in the 850-700mb \nlayer is aiding the precipitation. Models show the axis of \nconvergence moving southward steadily this afternoon and evening. \nExpecting the precipitation to come to an end by midnight. Low \nclouds will also trend southward and scour out along I-94 as we move \ninto the late evening hours.\n\nRainfall of 2-3 inches has been common in the heavy rain area to the \nsouth and southwest of Grand Rapids. Some reports in excess of 3 \ninches are also noted on local observation sites. \n\n- Mainly dry weather is expected from Monday through Friday\n\nHigh pressure is situated over the Great Lakes region for \nessentially the entire work week. Ensemble members of both the GFS \nand the ECWMF indicate mainly dry weather leading to a high \nconfidence forecast.\n\nThe only exception to this rule will be associated with an upper low \nseen spinning eastward across the state of Wyoming today. The upper \nlow will work through our area Tuesday and Wednesday. At this point \nwe have dry weather in areas that it traverses. The thinking is that \ntoo much dry air will be in place associated with the sprawling \nsurface high. We will be monitoring forecast trends, but at this \npoint at worst it would be a few isolated showers.\n\n- Seasonable temperatures this week...feeling more like fall\n\n850mb temperatures are expected to be in the single digits C this \nweek, which will equate to conditions feeling much more like fall. \nThe difference from this weekend to the upcoming work week is that \nit will be much less humid. Northeast winds via outflow from \nCanadian high pressure to the north will allow much drier air to \nfilter in. Dew points at present are around 60F in Grand Rapids. We \nexpect dew points to drop into the 30s and 40s F much of this week. \nThat is a drop in humidity that will be noticeable. Highs this \nweek will be in the 60s most days with lows in the 40s. For \nreference, normal high/low in Grand Rapids today 72 and 51. Bottom\nline...slightly cooler than normal temperatures combined with \nlower humidity will make for light jacket/sweatshirt weather.",
